  "notes": "Source: official Wichita State University College of Engineering '2024 - 2025 Computer Science Course Sequence' sheet (Catalog Term: Fall 2024, 120 total credits, last updated 1/30/2024), the edition for students required to take a First-Year Seminar. Hand-transcribed 2026-09-30 from the official PDF at the source_url above, which is linked as the newest Computer Science sequence on the official CoE degree-sheets page (https://www.wichita.edu/academics/engineering/advising/degree-sheets.php). Program: Bachelor of Science in Computer Science, School of Computing, College of Engineering, Wichita State University (Wichita, KS); ABET (CAC) accredited. Staleness: the 2024-2025 sheet is the newest official CS term-by-term sequence published (the CoE page lists 2025-2026 sheets for other majors but none for Computer Science). Known curriculum differences vs the current catalog edition: the current catalog lists CS 346 Introduction to Computer Networks and CS 356 Introduction to Computer Security, while this Fall 2024 sheet uses CS 664 Computer Networks and titles CS 356 'Introduction to Cybersecurity' (sheet title transcribed verbatim); prereqs were cross-checked against the archived 2024-2025 catalog CS course list and match. Sheet notes transcribed: (a) at least 12 of the 15 technical elective hours must be from the School of Computing or ECE; up to 2 credit hours of Coop courses may count as nondepartmental technical electives; the First-Year Seminar counts as a departmental (SoC) technical elective, giving 15 technical-elective hours total (FYS + 4 elective slots). (b) One of the two General Education slots must be Fine Arts and the other Social/Behavioral Sciences, both from the approved General Education list. (c) PHIL 354 fulfils the Humanities requirement. (d) CS 599 may only be taken after earning a C or better in CS 598 in exactly the previous long semester. Standing/grade rules from the sheet: PHIL 354 requires junior standing or departmental consent; CS 598 requires senior standing (CS 580 listed as prerequisite, PHIL 354 as concurrent prerequisite); all SoC/ECE prerequisite courses must be passed with a C or better. MATH 111 (or equivalent) is the sheet's prerequisite for ECE 194 and CS 211 and for MATH 242 placement ('See Course Catalog'); it is listed in external_prereqs since it is not part of the degree plan. IME 254 may alternatively use MATH 252 as prerequisite. 'Engineering+ Requirement (1/2/3 of 3)' is the College of Engineering's 3-part applied-learning requirement and carries 0 credits. Concurrent prerequisites (MATH 243 for PHYS 313; PHYS 314 for PHYS 316) are listed in the prereq arrays.",
